ultramodern: say where each thread keeps the processor

A recompiled thread's registers are a structure on its own host thread's stack.
Nothing else has ever needed to see one, because nothing else is that thread.

Saving the state of a running game needs to see all of them. The console's
memory is nearly all of the state -- the game's variables, its stacks, its
message queues -- but a thread parked inside a function left what it cares
about in registers, and a memory image put back without them resumes every
thread of the game holding numbers that belong to whatever the process was
doing when the image was taken.

So each thread writes down where its registers are while it is running, and
something outside the game can walk them. The map is keyed by the thread's own
OSThread, which is the one name for a thread that means the same thing in a
saved state as in the process reading one: it is an address in the game's
memory, and a game puts its threads in the same place every time it boots.

The registering is done in librecomp, which is where the registers are made;
this is the map and the two calls that maintain it.

--- a/ultramodern/src/threads.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/threads.cpp
@@ -1,7 +1,9 @@
 #include <cstdio>
 #include <thread>
 #include <cassert>
+#include <mutex>
 #include <string>
+#include <unordered_map>
 
 #include "ultramodern/ultra64.h"
 #include "ultramodern/ultramodern.hpp"
@@ -388,6 +390,51 @@
     return thread_self;
 }
 
+// --- where each thread keeps the processor -----------------------------------
+//
+// A thread's registers are a structure on its own host thread's stack, made by
+// `run_thread_function` and passed down through every recompiled function it
+// calls. Nothing else has ever needed to see one, because nothing else is that
+// thread.
+//
+// Saving the state of a running game needs to see all of them. The console's
+// memory holds nearly everything -- the game's variables, its stacks, its
+// message queues -- but a thread parked inside a function left the values it
+// cares about in registers, and a memory image put back without them resumes
+// every thread of the game holding numbers that belong to whatever the process
+// was doing at the moment the image was taken.
+//
+// So each thread writes down where its registers are while it runs. The map is
+// keyed by the thread's own OSThread, which is the name for a thread that means
+// the same thing in a saved state as it does in the process reading one: it is
+// an address in the game's memory, and the game puts its threads in the same
+// place every time it boots.
+static std::mutex thread_registers_mutex;
+static std::unordered_map<PTR(OSThread), void*> thread_registers;
+
+void ultramodern::set_thread_registers(PTR(OSThread) thread, void* registers) {
+    if (thread == NULLPTR) {
+        return;
+    }
+    std::lock_guard lock{ thread_registers_mutex };
+    thread_registers[thread] = registers;
+}
+
+void ultramodern::forget_thread_registers(PTR(OSThread) thread) {
+    if (thread == NULLPTR) {
+        return;
+    }
+    std::lock_guard lock{ thread_registers_mutex };
+    thread_registers.erase(thread);
+}
+
+void ultramodern::for_each_thread_registers(void (*visit)(PTR(OSThread), void*, void*), void* user) {
+    std::lock_guard lock{ thread_registers_mutex };
+    for (const auto& [thread, registers] : thread_registers) {
+        visit(thread, registers, user);
+    }
+}
+
 static std::thread thread_cleaner_thread;
 static moodycamel::BlockingConcurrentQueue<UltraThreadContext*> deleted_threads{};
 extern std::atomic_bool exited;
